    "story": "Coupling is the railroad's handshake, and like a handshake it can be done too hard. She backs toward the cars at a pace that looks like nothing, forty tons persuaded down to inches, and the man on the rear platform talks her in with one hand, the old language of circles and fists. The passengers who will ride these cars will never know this happened. They will feel it once, a small shudder through the floor, and think it was the track. It wasn't. It was two pieces of railroad deciding to be one, and a man close enough to the joint to know whether the decision held.",
    "notes": "The least spectacular and most skilled moment in railroading, caught at the speed it actually happens: the engine reversed, the coach dark and waiting, the whole negotiation carried by one man's raised hand. Compositionally the frame is all approach, two masses not yet joined, the gap between them the true subject. Collectors who have worked around couplers recognize the tension instantly; everyone else feels it without knowing why.",
